  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nConditions are calm and cool early this morning in central KY and \nsouthern IN. Temperatures are running mostly in the 40s, though the \ncoolest KY Mesonet stations (Meade, Harrison, and Cumberland \ncounties) have settled into the upper 30s. Some patchy frost remains \npossible in the coolest low-lying areas through sunrise. \n\nFog has been slow to develop this morning after dry air mixdown \nlowered dewpoints into the mid and upper 30s in many areas yesterday \nafternoon. However, the latest satellite imagery does reveal a \ngradual expansion of fog in the river valleys. Patchy dense fog will \nbe possible in the typical fog-prone areas during the morning \ncommute. Otherwise, another spectacular Fall day is on tap. We'll \nsee a few more high clouds, particularly over west-central and south-\ncentral KY this morning into early afternoon. After the crisp start, \ntemperatures will rebound into the lower 70s this afternoon. \n\nThrough the rest of this week, an amplifying upper level ridge over \nthe central CONUS will help keep active weather confined to the \nwestern US and Canada. High pressure at the surface generally \nremains in place off to our north through the weekend, helping to \nmaintain generally light NE flow and a dry airmass. Expect several \ndays of pleasant weather, with highs in the low to mid 70s and \nmorning lows in the 40s. Forecast confidence remains high in dry \nweather through at least Monday. \n\n===== Worsening Drought Conditions ====\n\nWhile the beautiful fall weather will be nice to have, it does come \nwith a catch. This prolonged dry period could worsen drought \nconditions for our area. Almost our entire forecast area is now \nexperiencing at least abnormally dry (D0) conditions, with south-\ncentral KY even worse with moderate drought (D1). Since Sept 1, \nLouisville (SDF) and Lexington are both running more than 2.7\" below \nnormal rainfall for this time frame. Bowling Green is only behind \n0.70\" since Sept 1, but the bigger picture shows they are 5.37\" \nbelow normal rainfall since the start of 2026. With no rainfall \nexpected in the next seven days, drought conditions are likely to \nworsen.",
